course: everyday
lesson: 392
title: Adults who help and adults who don't
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mrs Okafor keeps a little notebook, and one day she noticed something in it: the same group of boys ended up fighting every Friday, just after swimming, when everyone was tired, hungry and wet-haired. She moved the snack to before the bus, put Jake at the front with her, and the Friday fights simply stopped. Noticing a pattern is one of the cleverest things a grown-up can do.
explain: Not every grown-up helps. The dinner lady always said «sort it out yourselves», so the shoving in the queue went on for weeks. Kieran's gran shrugged «boys will be boys» about the street fights, as if bruises were a kind of weather. One teacher told Maddie she «can't help it, she's a tomboy», as if liking football meant she was bound to fight. Those words did not stop anything; they just told the children nobody was coming.
explain: Other grown-ups acted. Sam's mum rang the school the same evening Sam told her, and thanked Sam for telling. Lily's dad went to the school about the ponytail tugging, calmly, with dates written down. Jake's uncle took him to boxing club, where the first rule on the wall said «no fighting outside the ring — ever», and anyone who broke it was out. Jake said the coach was scarier than any Station Road boy.
explain: Mr Pryce invited the parents of both streets into the school hall, with tea and biscuits, to talk about the fights, and the community police officer started playing football on the rec on Saturday mornings with kids from both streets — he was terrible in goal and everyone loved it. If the first grown-up you tell doesn't help, that is not the end: there is always another one who will.
fact: A grown-up who notices when and where fights happen can often stop them by changing that time or place.
fact: Tiredness and hunger make tempers shorter for children and adults.
fact: «Sort it out yourselves» leaves the stronger or rougher child to win.
fact: «Boys will be boys» is an excuse, not a reason; boys can learn not to fight just like anyone else.
fact: Liking football or rough games does not make a girl, or anyone, bound to fight.
fact: When a child tells a parent, the most helpful parent listens, thanks them and contacts the school.
fact: Writing down what happened and when helps adults take a problem seriously.
fact: Good sports clubs, like boxing or judo clubs, have strict rules against fighting outside the club.
fact: Community police officers help keep neighbourhoods safe and often get to know local children.
fact: If one adult does not help, a child should tell another adult until someone does.
exercise: What pattern did Mrs Okafor notice? | The same boys fought every Friday after swimming | The fights only ever happened in the winter months | Only girls fought, and only on Mondays | Fights started in the library
exercise: How did Mrs Okafor stop the Friday fights? | She moved the snack earlier and kept Jake near her | She banned swimming for the whole class for ever | She shouted at the whole class | She sent the two boys home early every Friday
exercise: Why might children fight more after swimming? | They are tired, hungry and cranky | Water makes people angry for a while after | Swimming teaches you how to fight better | Chlorine in the pool makes boys rough
exercise: What did the dinner lady always say? | «Sort it out yourselves» | «Come and tell me next time» | «Let me write it all down» | «I'll call your parents tonight»
exercise: What happened because of the dinner lady's words? | The shoving went on for weeks | The shoving stopped at once and never came back | The children all became friends | The queue got much shorter after that
exercise: What did Kieran's gran say about street fights? | «Boys will be boys» | «Let me ring the school» | «Stay inside after dark» | «I'll talk to their parents»
exercise: Why is «boys will be boys» unhelpful? | It treats fighting as normal and stops nobody | It is a rule that comes from the police | It makes boys stop fighting much sooner than girls | It is true for every boy
exercise: What was wrong with the teacher telling Maddie she «can't help it, she's a tomboy»? | Liking football doesn't mean she has to fight | Tomboys really can't help fighting, it's who they are | The teacher should have said it louder | Maddie should stop playing football
exercise: What did Sam's mum do after Sam told her? | Thanked him and rang the school straight away | Told him to toughen up | Waited a month to see what happened | Went to shout at the boys
exercise: How did Lily's dad go to the school? | Calmly, with the dates written down | Angrily, shouting at the boys outside the gate | Secretly, so nobody knew | Only by sending a text to Lily
exercise: What was the boxing club's first rule? | No fighting outside the ring, ever | Hit the hardest you can so you win every time | Fight anyone who teases you | Only fight on weekends
exercise: How did Jake feel about the boxing coach? | He found the coach scarier than any Station Road boy | He thought the coach was soft and wouldn't really mind | He wanted to fight the coach | He never listened to him
exercise: What did Mr Pryce do about the street fights? | Invited both streets' parents to talk in the hall | Banned all children from playing on the rec | Told the two streets to fight it out once and for all | Moved the school to another town
exercise: What did the community police officer do? | Played Saturday football with kids from both streets | Arrested every child who had fought on the rec | Closed the rec for good and put a big lock on the gate | Only drove past in his car
exercise: Why did the kids love the officer in goal? | He was terrible and made everyone laugh | He never let any goals in, not a single one | He gave out sweets | He shouted at the other team the whole time
exercise: The first adult you tell does nothing. What should you do? | Tell another adult until someone helps | Decide that no adult can ever really help | Sort it out with fists | Keep it to yourself for ever
test: dinner lady: «not my problem, sort it urselves». What's wise? | Tell a teacher or another adult instead | Sort it out with a shove when nobody's looking | Give up on the queue | Sneak to the front
test: «my nan says boys will be boys, so fighting's fine». Is it? | No, it's an excuse; boys can learn not to fight | Yes, nans know best, they've seen it all | Yes, but only for boys | Only on the street, not at school
test: teacher to Maddie: «well ur a tomboy, what d'ya expect». What's true? | Liking football doesn't make anyone a fighter | Tomboys always fight, that's what makes them tomboys | Maddie should wear a dress | The teacher is being kind
test: Sam: «I told Mum and she rang school. Am I a grass now?» | No, telling Mum was the right thing | Yes, a big one, he'll be called a grass | Only if the boys ever find out who told | Yes, but it doesn't matter very much
test: Lily's dad wrote dates in a notebook before the meeting. Why? | So the school could see it kept happening | So he wouldn't forget to bring his coffee to the meeting | To make the meeting longer | To show off his handwriting
test: «boxing club? so ur gonna batter people now». What's true about a good club? | It has strict rules against fighting outside | It teaches you how to beat up anyone who annoys you | It's where street fights start | It has no rules at all
test: Every Friday at 3 the same lot kick off. What could a smart teacher change? | The time, place, or who stands near whom | Nothing, it's just Friday | Cancel Fridays | Give them a lot more homework so they're too busy
test: «police on the rec?? we're nicked lol». What was the officer actually doing? | Playing football and getting to know both streets | Arresting kids for playing football on the grass | Looking for a lost dog | Checking bikes for lights
test: Kieran's gran shrugs again. Who else could Kieran tell? | A teacher, Mr Pryce or the community officer | Nobody, Gran is the only one who ever listens | Only Jake | The Station Road boys
test: «what's the point telling, adults never do nothing». What's true? | Some don't, but many do, so keep telling | It's true, no adult ever helps, so don't bother | Only the police can help | Adults only help rich kids
test: Mr Pryce gets parents from both streets in for tea and biscuits. Why would that help? | Parents can agree together how to stop the fights | Biscuits make people forget all about the fights | It gets parents out of the house | It's just a party with tea and biscuits
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a member of staff who records the times and locations of altercations may prevent their recurrence. | A discernible pattern permits alteration of the circumstances that precede them | Records are of themselves a deterrent to all pupils who might fight | Pupils cease fighting once observed writing | Records are required solely for disciplinary exclusion from school
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression «boys will be boys» when applied to physical altercations? | It is an excuse which obstructs any remedy | It is an accurate account of the nature of boys | It constitutes official school policy on conduct | It is an appropriate response from a caring adult
exam: It is given that a pupil has reported mistreatment to an adult who has declined to act. Select the course of action most consistent with established guidance. | The pupil ought to report the matter to another adult until it is addressed | The pupil ought to resolve the matter by physical means | The pupil ought to refrain from all further reporting | The pupil ought to accept that the matter cannot be resolved by any adult at all
exam: State the conduct most conducive to the protection of a child who has disclosed mistreatment to a parent. | The parent listens, thanks the child and contacts the school | The parent instructs the child to retaliate | The parent defers all action for a month | The parent confronts the other children personally at the school gate
